There is no mathematics on this page. Folio 90 is a continuous prose passage of lecture or reading notes on ancient Greek history. It continues the account of the Peloponnesian War from the previous page and runs on to the next.

Content, paragraph by paragraph:
(1) The Peace of 421 could not last 'because individual men were too much interested in it [i.e. in the war]'.
(2) Character sketch of Alcibiades:
- son of Klinias, from one of the richest families;
- endowed by nature with everything that makes one popular in a democracy;
- educated with the greatest care by Pericles from his youth (the words 'auf war er' are added in the left margin);
- the most handsome man of his time, with irresistible eloquence;
- his vices as great as his virtues;
- a brilliant [mind?], but boundless vanity was the mainspring ('Feder') of all his actions.
(3) Political narrative:
- his ambition caused hostilities to be renewed, and he brought about Athens' alliance with Argos;
- from 419 the peace was broken insofar as the allies of both sides fought each other, and this lasted until 414;
- the battle of Mantinea in Arcadia, dated 417, brought the Argives over to Sparta's side;
- weaker states could no longer stay neutral, and Melos in 416 is the example.
(4) Alcibiades' grand plan to make Athens master of Sicily:
- Egesta sent envoys asking for help against Syracuse and Selinus;
- Athens had long had its eye on Sicily and judged the moment opportune, though it could scarcely defend itself.

The next page (91) continues with the Sicilian expedition of 415–413. There is nothing to reconstruct mathematically. The page is not mathematical work, and the hand and genre (dictated lecture notes on ancient history, of the Göttingen school-history type) suggest a student notebook bound into the bundle rather than Gauss's own research.
